An interesting consequence of Theorem 3.1 is this: By Theorem 6.4 of Chapter 2, we know that the gradient ∇g, when nonzero, is perpendicular to the level sets of g. 
Thus, the equation ∇f = λ∇g gives the condition for the normal vector to a level set of f to be parallel to that of a level set of g. 
Hence, for a point x₀ to be the site of an extremum of f on the level set S = {x | g(x) = c}, where ∇g(x₀) != 0, we must have that the level set R of f that contains x₀ is tangent to S at x₀. 

